# Supabase JS v2 Best Practices

## Client Setup

The Supabase client is at `src/integrations/supabase/client.ts` with generated types at `types.ts`.

```typescript
import { supabase } from "@/integrations/supabase/client";
import type { Database, Tables, TablesInsert, TablesUpdate } from "@/integrations/supabase/types";
```

### Type Safety

- Always use the generated `Database` type with `createClient<Database>()`
- Use `Tables<"table_name">` for row types, `TablesInsert<"table_name">` for inserts, `TablesUpdate<"table_name">` for updates
- Regenerate types after schema changes: `supabase gen types typescript --local > src/integrations/supabase/types.ts`

## Query Patterns

### Select with Type Inference

```typescript
// Typed select - let Supabase infer the return type
const { data, error } = await supabase
  .from("products")
  .select("id, name, price, category")
  .eq("category", "toys")
  .order("created_at", { ascending: false });

// With relations (foreign key joins)
const { data, error } = await supabase
  .from("cart_items")
  .select("*, product:products(*)")
  .eq("user_id", userId);
```

### Error Handling - ALWAYS Destructure and Check

```typescript
// CORRECT - always handle both data and error
const { data, error } = await supabase.from("products").select("*").eq("id", id).single();
if (error) {
  console.error("Failed to fetch product:", error.message);
  throw error;
}

// WRONG - ignoring error
const { data } = await supabase.from("products").select("*"); // error silently swallowed
```

### Use .maybeSingle() for Optional Rows

```typescript
// When the row might not exist (no error if 0 rows)
const { data, error } = await supabase
  .from("cart_items")
  .select("*")
  .eq("product_id", productId)
  .eq("user_id", userId)
  .maybeSingle(); // returns null if not found, no error

// .single() throws if 0 or 2+ rows — use only when exactly 1 row is guaranteed
```

### Batch Operations - Prefer Single Queries Over Loops

```typescript
// CORRECT - single query with .in()
const { data } = await supabase.from("products").select("*").in("id", productIds);

// WRONG - N+1 queries in a loop
for (const id of productIds) {
  const { data } = await supabase.from("products").select("*").eq("id", id).single();
}
```

### Upsert for Insert-or-Update

```typescript
const { error } = await supabase
  .from("profiles")
  .upsert({ id: userId, first_name: "John" }, { onConflict: "id" });
```

## Authentication

### Auth Listener Setup (do once at app root)

```typescript
// Set up auth state listener — returns cleanup function
const { data: { subscription } } = supabase.auth.onAuthStateChange((_event, session) => {
  // event: "SIGNED_IN" | "SIGNED_OUT" | "TOKEN_REFRESHED" | "USER_UPDATED" | "PASSWORD_RECOVERY"
  handleAuthChange(session);
});

// Cleanup on unmount
return () => subscription?.unsubscribe();
```

### Session Management

```typescript
// Get current session (reads from memory, fast)
const { data: { session } } = await supabase.auth.getSession();

// Get current user (makes network request to verify)
const { data: { user } } = await supabase.auth.getUser();

// Prefer getSession() for frequent checks, getUser() for verified identity
// In this project: AuthContext uses getSession() + onAuthStateChange listener
```

### Auth Performance Rules

- Call `supabase.auth.getUser()` sparingly — it makes a network request every time
- Cache user data in context/state after first fetch (this project uses `useAuthStatus` singleton)
- Use `supabase.auth.getSession()` for quick session checks (reads from local storage)
- Never call `getUser()` inside render or frequently-called callbacks

### Sign Out

```typescript
const { error } = await supabase.auth.signOut();
// Also clear local caches: localStorage, React Query, context state
```

## Realtime Subscriptions

### Channel Setup Pattern

```typescript
useEffect(() => {
  const channel = supabase
    .channel("unique-channel-name") // must be unique per subscription
    .on(
      "postgres_changes",
      {
        event: "*",       // or "INSERT" | "UPDATE" | "DELETE"
        schema: "public",
        table: "cart_items",
        filter: `user_id=eq.${userId}`,  // server-side filter to reduce traffic
      },
      (payload) => {
        // payload.new, payload.old, payload.eventType
        refetchData();
      }
    )
    .subscribe();

  return () => {
    supabase.removeChannel(channel); // ALWAYS clean up
  };
}, [userId]); // re-subscribe when userId changes
```

### Realtime Rules

- Always clean up channels in useEffect return
- Use server-side filters (`filter:`) to minimize payload
- Use unique channel names to avoid conflicts
- Don't subscribe to entire tables without filters in production
- Prefer invalidating React Query cache over manual state updates

## Edge Functions (Deno)

Edge Functions live in `supabase/functions/`. They run on Deno.

### Invoking from Client

```typescript
const { data, error } = await supabase.functions.invoke("function-name", {
  body: { action: "actionName", payload: { /* ... */ } },
});
if (error) throw error;
```

### Edge Function Structure

```typescript
import { serve } from "https://deno.land/std@0.168.0/http/server.ts";
import { createClient } from "https://esm.sh/@supabase/supabase-js@2";

serve(async (req) => {
  // CORS preflight
  if (req.method === "OPTIONS") {
    return new Response(null, { headers: corsHeaders });
  }

  try {
    const { action, payload } = await req.json();

    // Create Supabase client with service role for admin operations
    const supabaseAdmin = createClient(
      Deno.env.get("SUPABASE_URL")!,
      Deno.env.get("SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY")!
    );

    // ... handle action
    return new Response(JSON.stringify(result), {
      headers: { ...corsHeaders, "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    });
  } catch (error) {
    return new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: error.message }), {
      status: 400,
      headers: { ...corsHeaders, "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    });
  }
});
```

### Edge Function Environment Variables

```typescript
// Available automatically in Edge Functions:
Deno.env.get("SUPABASE_URL")
Deno.env.get("SUPABASE_ANON_KEY")
Deno.env.get("SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY")  // admin access — never expose to client

// Custom secrets (set via supabase secrets set):
Deno.env.get("SHIPPO_API_KEY")
Deno.env.get("STRIPE_SECRET_KEY")
```

### Edge Function Best Practices

- Use `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` only in Edge Functions, never in browser code
- Always return proper CORS headers
- Validate inputs before processing
- Use `fetchWithTimeout` for external API calls (as in `shipping/utils.ts`)
- Keep functions focused — one concern per function
- Handle errors with structured JSON responses

## React Query + Supabase Integration

### Query Hook Pattern (project standard)

```typescript
import { useSupabaseQuery } from "@/hooks/useDataFetching";

function useProducts() {
  return useSupabaseQuery(
    ["products"],           // query key
    "products",             // table name (for batching)
    () => supabase.from("products").select("*").order("created_at", { ascending: false }),
    { staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000 }
  );
}
```

### Mutation Pattern

```typescript
import { useMutation, useQueryClient } from "@tanstack/react-query";

function useDeleteProduct() {
  const queryClient = useQueryClient();

  return useMutation({
    mutationFn: async (productId: string) => {
      const { error } = await supabase.from("products").delete().eq("id", productId);
      if (error) throw error;
    },
    onSuccess: () => {
      queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ["products"] });
    },
  });
}
```

### Cache Invalidation After Auth Changes

```typescript
// Invalidate user-specific caches when auth state changes
queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ["cart"] });
queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ["favorites"] });
queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ["profile"] });
```

## Security Checklist

- Never expose `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` in client code
- Supabase anon key is safe for client (it's public by design, RLS enforces access)
- Always use Row Level Security (RLS) on tables accessed from the browser
- Use Edge Functions for operations requiring elevated privileges
- Validate all user inputs server-side (Edge Functions) before database operations
- Use `.eq("user_id", userId)` filters on queries to scope data to current user
